export const AGENTS_MARKDOWN = "# AQUA agent guide\n\nAQUA is founder-first application infrastructure for application answer reuse: write strong answers to common application questions once and adapt them across accelerator, fellowship, grant, job and school applications. Questions, answers, versions, evidence and review history stay connected by lineage. AQUA is local-first: private answers stay on the user's device.\n\nThis page is for AI agents and the people who run them. It describes the only supported way for an agent to help a user finish an application: the local AQUA companion. Machine-readable versions: https://aquaidp.xyz/llms.txt, https://aquaidp.xyz/llms-full.txt, https://aquaidp.xyz/.well-known/mcp.\n\n## The agent path: the local AQUA companion\n\nAQUA is local-first. A user's private answers live in an encrypted vault in their browser and, if they choose, in an encrypted companion workspace folder on their own computer. They are never stored on AQUA's servers, and no AQUA service hands them to an agent. An agent helps a user by running the **AQUA companion** on the user's machine: a command-line tool and stdio MCP server that works on one consented, encrypted workspace plus the public catalog.\n\n### 1. Install\n- Today: the companion ships as a versioned tarball from this site (Node.js 22+, no dependencies):\n  `npx --yes https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/aqua-companion-0.2.0.tgz --help`\n- sha256: `68b16804806e3405afa6182ae2bd450d3018bfbd07e898b9e03a59f212c2e217` (also at https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/aqua-companion-0.2.0.tgz.sha256; https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/latest.json lists the current version).\n- npm: not yet on npm: publishing aqua-companion is owner gate GB2. Until then install from the tarball above.\n- `aqua-mcp-server`: aqua-mcp-server 2.0.0 is built on the companion and is not yet published. Once OWNER publishes it (gate GB1), `npx -y aqua-mcp-server` runs it; until then use the companion tarball. Versions below 2.0 are legacy and must not be used.\n- Self-check: `npx --yes https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/aqua-companion-0.2.0.tgz doctor` reports configuration, catalog connectivity, workspace and keychain status.\n\n### 2. Connect it to the agent (MCP over stdio)\n```\nclaude mcp add aqua -- npx --yes https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/aqua-companion-0.2.0.tgz mcp --keychain\n```\nThe server starts even when no workspace exists yet. Workspace tools then return `workspace_not_initialized` with next steps.\n\n### 3. Workspace setup and consent\n- `locate_workspace` (read) reports where a workspace is or would be created.\n- `init_workspace` (human_consent) uses MCP elicitation when the client supports it: the user confirms the folder and consent in the client UI, outside the model. Otherwise it returns `requires_human` with the exact command, for example `aqua-companion init --path <folder>`.\n- The workspace passphrase is typed by the user (terminal prompt, macOS Keychain or the server's own environment). It never travels through the model or a tool argument, and no tool accepts a password, token or JWT.\n- The agent reads files only from the workspace `inbox/` folder. Ask the user to place files there.\n\n### 4. Tiers (enforced in code, not by asking the agent to behave)\n- **read** (23): Always allowed. Only reads the workspace or the public catalog cache.\n- **private_write** (8): Stages private records in the user's local workspace (drafts, imports, evidence). Refused in --read-only mode. Never approves anything.\n- **human_consent** (1): Acts only after the human confirms in the MCP client UI (elicitation). Without that capability it returns requires_human with the exact command for the user to run.\n- **human_only** (5): Never acts. Returns requires_human with the steps the user takes in the AQUA browser app (approve, sign, freeze/export, submit, attest).\n\n### 5. Submitted history\nThe user copies past submitted applications (md, txt, docx, html, json, csv) into `inbox/`. `import_history` (or `aqua-companion import-history <folder>`) stages each answer with the exact question wording, program, source file hash and submission date. Unknown dates are flagged, never guessed. After `aqua-companion export-backup` and Restore in the browser, the user confirms them at https://aquaidp.xyz/history. Confirmed answers count as approved as of their submission date. Answers with unfilled placeholders ([CONFIRM], [ASSET NEEDED], TODO, TBD, XXX, <…>, {{…}}) can never be confirmed; the vault refuses them.\n\n### 6. The human approves in the browser\nDrafts the agent stages are unapproved. Approval binds the exact version hash and happens only in the AQUA browser review board, opened from https://aquaidp.xyz/aqua-local after Restore. The approval round trip:\n1. The user runs `aqua-companion export-backup <file>`.\n2. In the browser: AQUA Local, Restore backup, review and approve.\n3. The user exports a browser backup and puts it in the workspace `inbox/`.\n4. The agent runs `sync_from_vault_export`; `read_review_status` then shows the human approvals with hashes preserved.\n5. `prepare_packet` reports readiness; the user freezes and exports the packet in the browser; `verify_packet` checks the export. The agent never submits anything.\n\n### 7. Public catalog configuration\nhttps://aquaidp.xyz/.well-known/aqua-catalog.json returns `{version, supabase_url, publishable_key, tables, notes}`. The key is the public publishable/anon key the web app already ships to every browser; row-level security limits it to approved canonical catalog rows. Clients must publishable/anon key only; clients refuse any other role and fetch it only from https://aquaidp.xyz (or a local override file).\n\n### 8. Hosted read-only MCP\nhttps://aquaidp.xyz/mcp: live (read-only public catalog tools, resources and prompts; no authentication, no workspace tools; rate-limited).\n\n### 9. Intentionally absent\n- `publish_catalog_entry`: Catalog publication needs reviewer authorization and source verification in the AQUA service; the agent can only prepare a candidate.\n- `run_migration`: Database migrations change production permissions and require explicit owner authorization.\n- `admin_platform`: Platform administration (users, roles, billing, teams) is not a user capability.\n- `configure_security`: Security configuration (RLS, keys, service-role access) is owner-only and never handled by agents.\n- `read_hosted_answers`: Private answers live in the browser vault and this workspace; the agent never gets database or service-role access.\n- `TRANSPORT=http (self-hosted HTTP)`: 1.x served unauthenticated write-capable HTTP. 2.0 has stdio locally and the read-only hosted endpoint only.\n- There is no service-role key, no account token and no hosted private-answer tool anywhere in the agent path.\n\n### Tools (37 tools, from application-hub-mcp-server/TOOLS.json)\n| Tool | Tier | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `approve_answer` | human_only | Never executes. |\n| `attach_evidence` | private_write | Stage an evidence record for a file the user placed in inbox/ (its sha256 is recorded) and optionally link it to a version. |\n| `attest_submission` | human_only | Never executes. |\n| `create_draft_version` | private_write | Stage a NEW, unapproved draft version of an answer in this workspace (method recorded as an agent draft). |\n| `discover_applications` | read | Search the cached public AQUA catalog (approved canonical programs) and list the applications in this workspace. |\n| `doctor` | read | Self-check: Node and companion version, the public catalog configuration (source, origin, key role: only anon/publishable is accepted), connectivity and a canonical-only catalog read (counts only), workspace and macOS… |\n| `export_packet` | human_only | Never executes. |\n| `get_application_details` | read | Full template of one application: exact question wording, parsed requirements (limits, required, time context, qualifiers), deadline and time zone, and where the data came from and how it was verified. |\n| `hub_find_similar_questions` | read | Find canonical questions in the approved catalog that ask about the same thing as the given text (lexical similarity over question wording and its recorded variants; no embeddings, nothing stored). |\n| `hub_get_acceptance_stats` | read | Public acceptance figures for one approved canonical program: acceptance rate and application count where public, and counts of verified applicant-reported outcomes by cohort (counts only, never who reported). |\n| `hub_get_heat_scores` | read | Approved canonical programs ranked by heat score (current applicant interest and deadline pressure). |\n| `hub_get_program_by_slug` | read | One approved canonical program by slug with its public stats and theme weights (DNA) in a single call. |\n| `hub_get_program_detail` | read | Full public detail for one approved canonical program by program_id or slug: deadlines, cycles, rolling/cohort, value and acceptance figures where public. |\n| `hub_get_program_dna` | read | Theme weight breakdown of one approved canonical program (what it emphasizes, from its questions), by program_id or slug. |\n| `hub_get_program_questions` | read | The application questions of one approved canonical program in display order: exact wording (in untrusted_content), word and character limits, required flag, theme, and how many approved programs ask the same canonica… |\n| `hub_get_program_rankings` | read | Approved canonical programs ranked by program value score (what you get versus what you give up, relative to application effort). |\n| `hub_get_question_significance` | read | How much one canonical question matters across approved programs: how many ask it and what share of approved programs that is, typical limits, theme, and which programs ask it. |\n| `hub_get_universal_questions` | read | Canonical questions asked by most approved programs: the ones to answer first. |\n| `hub_search_programs` | read | Search approved canonical AQUA programs (accelerators, grants, fellowships, VC and other programs) by words, type, status, equity, cash, rolling and deadline window. |\n| `import_history` | private_write | Batch-stage applications the user ALREADY SUBMITTED (a folder inside the workspace inbox/, .md .txt .docx .html .json .csv) as history records: exact question wording, the submitted answer, program, source file sha256… |\n| `import_local_application` | private_write | Stage a private local application from a file the user placed in the workspace inbox/ folder (.txt, .md, .html, .csv, .json, .docx). |\n| `init_workspace` | human_consent | Create an encrypted AQUA workspace ONLY after the user confirms the folder and consent in the MCP client UI (elicitation). |\n| `inspect_extracted_questions` | read | Show how the questions of a workspace application were extracted: exact wording, parsed requirements, source location, and items the parser was unsure about. |\n| `locate_workspace` | read | Where the AQUA workspace is (or would be created): whether this server has one open, the configured path, the folders searched, and the exact init command. |\n| `match_canonical_questions` | read | Match an application's questions (or given question texts) against canonical questions in the cached public catalog with the tiered matcher (exact, normalized, semantic, novel). |\n| `prepare_catalog_candidate` | private_write | Build a sanitized public catalog candidate (program, deadlines, public question wording only) from a staged application imported with a public source_url. |\n| `prepare_packet` | read | Readiness report for freezing an application packet: per question the selectable approved version, whether its approval is bound to the exact hash, length limits, required questions, and content integrity. |\n| `present_review_decisions` | read | Build the offline AQUA review file (HTML) for one application from workspace data and save it under exports/reviews/ (skipped in read-only mode). |\n| `read_review_status` | read | Derived review status of every question in an application (gap, suggested, needs evidence, under review, approved, ...) and the Direct / Adapt / Gap reuse recommendation against the rest of the answer bank. |\n| `record_observed_submission` | private_write | Store an observation that an application may have been submitted (for example a confirmation e-mail the user saved in inbox/), flagged requires_human_attestation. |\n| `refresh_catalog` | read | Fetch approved canonical programs, cycles and questions from the configured public AQUA catalog endpoint (read-only, public key, nothing from the workspace is sent). |\n| `request_evidence` | private_write | Record an evidence gap on a version (what support is missing). |\n| `search_answer_bank` | read | Search the user's private answers, versions, facts and evidence in this workspace (decrypted in memory only). |\n| `sign_packet` | human_only | Never executes. |\n| `submit_catalog_candidate` | human_only | Never executes. |\n| `sync_from_vault_export` | private_write | Import an encrypted backup the user exported from the AQUA browser vault and placed in the workspace inbox/ (the same checks as \"aqua-companion import-backup\": integrity, account binding, conflicts recorded, nothing o… |\n| `verify_packet` | read | Verify an exported AQUA packet JSON placed in inbox/ (or packets/): canonical manifest, manifest hash, packet id, per-answer hashes, provenance receipts and the signature if present. |\n\n## The fresh-agent journey\n1. **Install**: npx --yes https://aquaidp.xyz/companion/aqua-companion-0.2.0.tgz --help (check the published sha256 first)\n2. **Set up a workspace**: init_workspace (the user confirms the folder in the MCP client) or the user runs `aqua-companion init --path <folder>`; the passphrase is typed by the user, never sent through the model\n3. **Load submitted history**: the user puts past submitted applications in the workspace inbox/; import_history stages them; the user exports a backup and confirms them at https://aquaidp.xyz/history\n4. **Ingest the application**: import_local_application (a file in inbox/) or get_application_details for a catalog program\n5. **Match and reuse**: match_canonical_questions, search_answer_bank, read_review_status (Direct / Adapt / Gap)\n6. **Draft**: create_draft_version stages unapproved drafts; request_evidence / attach_evidence\n7. **Human review and approval**: the user runs export-backup, restores it at https://aquaidp.xyz/aqua-local and approves each answer on the browser review board\n8. **Return approvals to the agent**: the user exports a browser backup into inbox/; sync_from_vault_export imports it; read_review_status shows approved with the hash preserved\n9. **Packet**: prepare_packet (readiness), the user freezes/exports in the browser, verify_packet checks the export. Nothing is ever submitted by the agent.\n\n## Contribution Exchange\nThe Contribution Exchange is not part of AQUA. It lives at https://signalaf.com/exchange.\n"
